WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.214
The serving runtime turns model files and accelerator resources into a network service.

00:00:06.214 --> 00:00:07.286
Llama.

00:00:07.286 --> 00:00:14.357
cpp server is portable and effective for quantized models across CPU and several GPU backends.

00:00:14.357 --> 00:00:18.857
Ollama packages model management behind a straightforward local service.

00:00:18.857 --> 00:00:27.214
These tools are useful for labs and modest deployments where operational simplicity matters more than maximizing concurrent throughput.

00:00:27.214 --> 00:00:36.428
VLLM is designed for high-throughput language-model serving and includes memory-management and batching techniques suited to concurrent requests on supported hardware.

00:00:36.428 --> 00:00:43.500
Text Generation Inference, often called TGI, is another production-oriented server with model-serving and observability features.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.285
Their advantages appear only when models, accelerators, and traffic patterns are compatible.

00:00:49.285 --> 00:00:58.928
Benchmark the actual model and prompt distribution; do not select a runtime solely from an impressive result produced on different hardware.

00:00:58.928 --> 00:01:03.857
An OpenAI-compatible API can decouple clients from the underlying server.

00:01:03.857 --> 00:01:10.928
Applications use a familiar request format while a gateway or router directs traffic to llama.

00:01:10.928 --> 00:01:14.142
cpp, Ollama, VLLM, or another backend.

00:01:14.142 --> 00:01:24.214
Compatibility is not always identical across parameters, streaming, tool calls, embeddings, and error behavior, so test the features your clients depend on.

00:01:24.214 --> 00:01:34.285
Open WebUI can provide a shared browser front end, but it should use the same authentication and routing controls as other clients.

00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:38.785
Introduce model routing only after one server is reliable.

00:01:38.785 --> 00:01:47.142
Start a known model, confirm acceleration, measure latency and throughput, exercise health checks, and document startup and shutdown.

00:01:47.142 --> 00:01:51.642
Then replicate the service or add another model role.

00:01:51.642 --> 00:01:57.856
Complex orchestration cannot repair an unstable serving process; it only distributes the instability.

00:01:57.856 --> 00:02:04.070
A simple, observable serving layer is the foundation for safe scheduling and scale.
